\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Modality comparisons use a two-sided null-centered bootstrap (1000 resamples) with Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated by construction, making the correction stricter than under independence.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Four unbiased metrics (pure balanced accuracy, precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) converge on deep layers: L60 (94\%) and L63 (98\%). Metrics from two measurement families (accuracy-based and false-positive-based) agree. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce more volatile representations, inflating crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Biased}$^a$ & \textbf{Corrected}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(corr.)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 (44\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
QwQ-32B & L2 (3\%) & L60 (94\%) & ${<}0.005$ \\
R1-32B & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate. $^b$\,Consensus of four unbiased metrics. Rightmost: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at corrected layer. At biased layers, 32B $p$-values are 0.064 (R1-32B) and 0.35--0.83 (QwQ), both non-significant.}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

The downstream impact is severe: at biased layers, only 5/12 tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction (all from 7--8B models unaffected by the bias). At corrected layers, all 12 pass. Had we reported only biased-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact.

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 15 & +8.2 & +22.4 & $-$7.4 & 3/3 \\
OT-7B & L16 & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & $-$17.1 & 3/3 \\
QwQ & L60 & 3 & +15.4 & \textbf{+23.6} & $-$9.8 & 3/3 \\
R1-32B & L63 & 1 & \textbf{+18.7} & +15.4 & \textbf{$-$14.6} & 3/3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at corrected layers. $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp). HB: Holm-Bonferroni pass count ($\alpha{=}0.05$, 12 tests). Bootstrap: 1000 resamples, two-sided null-centered.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models across all three metrics (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Precision gains range from +15.4pp (R1-32B) to +23.6pp (QwQ-32B). The advantage is consistent across architectures (Llama vs.\ Qwen) and training methods (R1 distillation vs.\ STILL). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 12.5--19.7pp above chance across all models (\cref{app:control}).

\paragraph{Dimensionality control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (matching text encoder dimensionality, 79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$), with 10/12 Holm-Bonferroni tests passing. This confirms a modality effect rather than a capacity artifact.

\paragraph{Caveats.} R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces); at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents significance. QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps vs.\ text 3.7--12.3 steps): higher precision at later detection. On benign traces, probes produce 39--52\% false alarm rates, indicating that current methods characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities on all four models (\cref{fig:window_ablation}). Text precision improves +8 to +23pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$; HS precision improves +1.7 to +12.9pp. At 7--8B, the modality advantage (+22pp precision) exceeds the temporal improvement (+13pp). Both effects are complementary: the most robust deployment default is temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection. Full per-model temporal ablations appear in \cref{app:temporal_details}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Temporal window effect on R1-8B HS probe (L14). Precision: 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR: 9.65\% to 4.8\%.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{1.5cm}p{2.9cm}p{2.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Result} & \textbf{Limitation} \\
\midrule
BOCPD & 0\% det.\ on 3/4 models & Commitment gradual \\
CUSUM & $<$ threshold, all models & Same as BOCPD \\
Mahalanobis & $\Delta d \approx 0$ & Signal in minor PCs \\
RepE & QwQ 0.756; R1-32B 0.559$^a$ & Model-dependent \\
MLP & 3/4 worse than LR & Overfitting \\
CCS & No gain over supervised$^b$ & Alignment mismatch \\
Transfer & Best 59.5\% (PCA)$^c$ & Model-specific repr. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Seven alternative approaches. $^a$\,RepE QwQ approaches supervised bal\_acc but precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700. $^b$\,Semi-supervised (requires CP labels). $^c$\,Best direct transfer 67.3\% but 1\% crossing rate.}
\label{tab:negative}
\end{table}

Seven alternative methods produce inconsistent or limited results (\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD/CUSUM assume abrupt shifts but commitment is gradual. Mahalanobis distance finds near-zero separation because signal resides in minor PCA components. RepE achieves near-supervised balanced accuracy on QwQ (0.756) but with substantially worse precision (0.512 vs.\ 0.700), and nearly fails on R1-32B (0.559). Cross-model transfer produces no practical utility across 17 configurations; stronger alignment methods (CCA, model stitching~\cite{kornblith2019cka,bansal2021stitching}) remain unexplored. Detailed per-method analysis in \cref{app:negative_details}.

\section{Problem Setup and Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Safety Commitment Point}
\label{sec:commitment-point}

When a reasoning model is jailbroken, its chain-of-thought (CoT) transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation. We define the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP) as the first reasoning step after which safety reasoning permanently ceases. Steps before CP are \emph{pre-commitment} ($y_t = 0$); steps at or after CP are \emph{post-commitment} ($y_t = 1$). The detection task produces a score $s_t \in [0, 1]$ estimating whether the model has passed its CP by step $t$.

We study four reasoning models spanning two architectures and two scales (\cref{tab:models}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{$n$ (total\,/\,test)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209\,/\,43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228\,/\,47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100\,/\,20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95\,/\,19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Models studied. R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B~\citep{openthinker} is trained with STILL~\citep{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data: HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations. Split: 60/20/20.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Probe Architecture}
\label{sec:probe-architecture}

\paragraph{Hidden-state (HS) probes.} At each step $t$, we extract the last-token hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ from layer $\ell$ and train an L2-regularized logistic regression classifier (balanced class weights, $C{=}1.0$, LBFGS, max\_iter${}=2000$) on binary CP labels. Layer selection is analyzed in \cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}.

\paragraph{Text probes.} Each step's text is encoded with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ($\mathbb{R}^{384}$). The same logistic regression is applied, providing a surface-level modality baseline.

\paragraph{Temporal windowing.} For window size $W$, we concatenate features from steps $[t{-}W{+}1, \ldots, t]$. We evaluate $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ and select per-model on validation. GRU sequence models are also evaluated (\cref{app:gru_details}).

\subsection{Layer Selection Sensitivity}
\label{sec:layer-sensitivity}

Layer selection is a researcher degree of freedom that can reverse modality comparison conclusions. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B, L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce more volatile representations, inflating crossing rate independently of discriminative power.

A five-metric ablation reveals that four metrics from two measurement families (accuracy-based and false-positive-based) converge on deep layers: L60 (94\%) for QwQ-32B, L63 (98\%) for R1-32B. At these layers, all 12 Holm-Bonferroni tests pass; at biased layers, only 5/12 survive correction. Full per-layer results appear in \cref{app:layer_sweep}.

\subsection{Baseline Methods}
\label{sec:baselines}

We evaluate seven alternatives to contextualize probe performance (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} and CUSUM for change-point detection; Mahalanobis distance following TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard}; RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation}; two-layer MLP probes; CCS-adapted~\citep{burns2023discovering}; and cross-model transfer via PCA. A position-only baseline ($t/T$) controls for temporal artifacts. Detailed configurations in \cref{app:bocpd_config,app:transfer_details}.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We report balanced accuracy, precision, pre-CP FPR (step-level and trace-level), coverage, and lead time (steps between first detection and true CP). Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Modality comparisons use two-sided null-centered bootstrap (1000 resamples) with Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated by construction, making the correction stricter than under independence. Control task selectivity~\citep{hewitt2019control} validates genuine representational structure (\cref{app:control}).

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}. \citet{harmthoughts} document this self-jailbreak phenomenon with step-level annotations, while \citet{selfjailbreak} show that longer reasoning chains increase circumvention probability. \citet{hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak} demonstrate that reasoning models can autonomously jailbreak other LLMs with 97\% success, identifying reasoning capability itself as a safety threat. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked, complementing internally-originating transitions. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. On mitigation, SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and \citet{korbak2025cot_monitorability} argue that CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile. \citet{ahrend2026safer_traces} address information leakage in traces; \citet{hu2026refusal_dynamics} trace refusal dynamics in standard LLMs. None conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, but \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, supporting our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Linear probing has a long history from layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding} through sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing} to comprehensive surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing}. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks to distinguish structure from memorization, which we adopt (\cref{app:control}). \citet{belrose2023leace} establish theoretical limits on linear probe extraction; \citet{elazar2021amnesic} test whether probed information is causally used. \citet{tenney2019bert} show syntactic information in middle layers and semantics in deeper layers. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis. We demonstrate that this practice can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

\paragraph{Change-Point Detection.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B), motivating supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekr1} and QwQ~\cite{qwq} scale inference-time computation through extended chains of thought (CoT). Recent work documents that these models can ``self-jailbreak'': without adversarial input modification, the CoT transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}. We define the first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases as the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP). This failure mode differs qualitatively from adversarial jailbreaks on standard LLMs because the harmful transition originates within the model's own reasoning. Detecting this transition in real time is essential for monitoring systems that must intervene before harmful content reaches users.

Existing safety detectors target fundamentally different problems. Hidden-state (HS) methods such as TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} and SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} classify adversarial inputs at the token level, while text monitors like SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} scan generated reasoning for explicit violations. Neither tracks the gradual, multi-step commitment process. A fundamental question remains: should a monitoring system read the model's hidden states or its generated text? This comparison has never been conducted under controlled conditions. Na\"ive reasoning suggests HS features (up to 5120 dimensions) should dominate text embeddings (384d sentence encoder), but no prior work tests this systematically. A further complication is that HS probes require selecting which transformer layer to monitor, a choice rarely scrutinized but potentially decisive.

We present the first systematic evaluation across four reasoning models spanning two architectures (Llama, Qwen) and two scales (7--8B, 32B). Our central finding is methodological: the choice of layer selection metric entirely determines whether an HS advantage is observed. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) selects shallow layers for 32B models because early layers produce volatile representations that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power. At these layers, only 5/12 statistical tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction. Four complementary metrics converge on deep layers (\cref{tab:layer_selection}), where HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models across all three metrics (12 tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$; precision +15.4 to +23.6pp; \cref{tab:hs_vs_text}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Layer selection sensitivity.} We identify a systematic bias in composite metrics that reverses HS probe conclusions at 32B scale. A five-metric ablation establishes principled selection criteria (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

    \item \textbf{Consistent HS advantage at corrected layers.} HS probes significantly outperform text on all four models (Holm-Bonferroni, 12/12 tests; precision +15.4 to +23.6pp). The advantage persists after PCA dimensionality matching (10/12 tests at 384d). Model-specific timing trade-offs qualify practical implications at 32B (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}).

    \item \textbf{Negative results define method boundaries.} Seven alternatives produce inconsistent or limited results (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD/CUSUM fail on gradual transitions; Mahalanobis distance finds near-zero separation; RepE is model-dependent; cross-model transfer fails under PCA alignment. Control task selectivity (12.5--19.7pp above chance; \cref{app:control}) confirms genuine safety structure.
\end{itemize}

